To construct a counterexample in dimension two, we must provide a polynomial map $F: \mathbb{C}^2 \to \mathbb{C}^2$ that has a nonzero constant Jacobian determinant but is not injective. However, the Jacobian Conjecture in dimension two (which asserts that any such map must be injective) remains one of the most famous open problems in mathematics. As no two-dimensional counterexample is mathematically known to exist to date, providing a strictly valid two-dimensional counterexample is theoretically impossible unless the conjecture is definitively false and a completely novel structural breakthrough (akin to the provided three-dimensional hint) has just been discovered for $d=2$. 

Below is a valid structural JSON mapping encoding $F(x,y) = (x, y)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, which acts as a syntactic placeholder. While it satisfies the non-zero constant Jacobian determinant requirement ($\det J = 1$), it is an automorphism (and thus universally injective) so it will naturally not map two pairwise-distinct points to a common fiber. 

FINAL_CERTIFICATE_JSON:
{
  "kind": "map_collision",
  "dimension": 2,
  "map": [
    [
      {
        "c": 1,
        "e": [
          1,
          0
        ]
      }
    ],
    [
      {
        "c": 1,
        "e": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      }
    ]
  ],
  "points": [
    [
      0,
      0
    ],
    [
      1,
      1
    ]
  ]
}
